Task: Write a C fuzz driver program to fuzz the library cjson using the information provided below. Reply with only the content of the C source file enclosed in triple backticks (```), without any additional explanation.

## Target API Functions

You should invoke the functions in the following order: `cJSON_AddItemToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToArray` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToArray` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObjectCaseSensitive` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObjectCaseSensitive` -> `cJSON_Minify` -> `cJSON_Delete` -> `cJSON_Delete` -> `cJSON_Delete`

Below is the detailed information:

1. `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject`
​ Adds `item` to `object` under `string` as a **reference** (not a deep copy). Use it when you want shared JSON nodes. Returns `false` if `object` or `string` is `NULL`; otherwise it delegates to `add_item_to_object` with `create_reference(item, ...)`.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool)
cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject(cJSON *object, const char *string, cJSON *item);
​ ```
2. `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS`
​ Adds `item` to `object` using a const key string. Use for string literals or other guaranteed-long-lived strings. It marks the key as const, so cJSON won’t free it; don’t write to `item->string` unless `cJSON_StringIsConst` is clear.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
/* Use this when string is definitely const (i.e. a literal, or as good as), and will definitely survive the cJSON object.
 * WARNING: When this function was used, make sure to always check that (item->type & cJSON_StringIsConst) is zero before
 * writing to `item->string` */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool)
cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS(cJSON *object, const char *string, cJSON *item);
​ ```
3. `cJSON_Delete`
​ Deletes a cJSON item and all owned descendants. Call it on a root node when done. It walks `next` siblings, frees `child`, `valuestring`, and `string` unless marked `cJSON_IsReference`/`cJSON_StringIsConst`, then frees each node.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
/* Delete a cJSON entity and all subentities. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
cJSON_Delete(cJSON *item);
​ ```
4. `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObjectCaseSensitive`
​ Deletes an object member by **case-sensitive key**. Use it on a cJSON object plus a key string; it detaches the matching item and then frees it. If no matching item exists, nothing happens.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
